# Per-call trig bench overlay (main/scene/fxbench.c).
# NOT part of sdkconfig.defaults -- that file is shared by every build_*
# directory in this tree (see CLAUDE.md), and this option must stay off there.
#
# Same isolation trap as sdkconfig.vmprobe.defaults: ESP-IDF puts sdkconfig at
# the project root by default REGARDLESS of -B, so this needs its own
# -D SDKCONFIG=<dir>/sdkconfig or it silently turns every other build_*
# directory's next build into a bench build too.
#
#   idf.py -B build_fxbench \
#       -D SDKCONFIG=build_fxbench/sdkconfig \
#       -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.fxbench.defaults" build
#
# Why the bench exists at all: the frame instruments (PERF/SPLIT3 and the
# once-per-window switch flips) cannot answer this one. The trig change has no
# runtime switch to flip -- fx_sinf *is* the call -- and its expected frame
# effect (216.8 calls a frame in solar_sail, 57 instructions saved a call, so
# ~12,400 instructions, ~0.05 ms) is under the SPLIT3 noise floor. What can be
# measured in one binary is the cost of one call, which is what this times.
#
# Note what it costs: sinf/cosf/tanf come back into the image (6,789 bytes of
# compiler_builtins) to have a third arm, so this build's flash is NOT
# comparable with a shipping build's. The arms are:
#   lut    the shipping implementation (fxmath.c, Q31 table + Lagrange)
#   poly   c66f401's degree-5 polynomial, copied into fxbench.c and proven
#          bit-identical by tools/test_fxmath_poly.c
#   libm   sinf/cosf/tanf
CONFIG_POCKET_FX_BENCH=y
